ound that Turner before I became an  artist he was my hero  and I always look to him as the  Braveheart type figure in our community  that was forgotten that history kind of  turn  they're had away from someone I became  you know and when I started to write the  first story I felt that was that I  thought was worthy kind of the elevate  me from the roles of like [ __ ] number  one or blood number two I thought maybe  this character was someone were  supported celebrating and exploring  Penelope and Asia a lot has been written  about NAT not all of it is to be trusted  but there's a there's a record there  with your characters who are historical  characters who lived and died how much  was known about them and where did you  find yourself having to fill in the  blanks and what were the conversations  you had with Nate about what those  blanks might be Penelope I'll start with  you well you should probably found a  little more than I did it was very hard  to find anything about Elizabeth we knew  she lived and existed and was the master  of the home and that that they they her  husband was a preacher and and so you  know I knew a lot about the history of  that period and and Nate and I spoke a  lot about it and we had packets and we  learned a lot about that time and of  course about nat turner but in order to  play a character as an actor i mean  there's not going to be a lot written  about her personality and it's not like  Mary Todd Lincoln where I could read a  lot about her and find out all about her  character so really I had to draw upon  the relationships that Nate wrote in the  story and really had to create my own  sense of who she was and what she was  about and really had to go to my  director and the writer and really go  into that and and find out really what  what I was going to do to make her real  and authentic and so it was um it was  sort of a combination of a lot of things  for me obviously understanding that time  period and women you know living 